On the real line, nothing says that a point is unknown: Lebesgue measure has infinite mass, and a
normal with a large variance is the usual stand-in. On a compact manifold the statement has an
exact form, the *uniform distribution*, and the stand-in fails: a
[normal](../normal/) with a large scale is not nearly uniform, and becomes less so as its scale
grows. This note defines the uniform distribution, reads it in a tangent chart, where it turns out
to be a Student t, says how a uniform point is drawn, and shows what a normal with a large scale
is instead.

## The definition

Let $\manifold$ be a manifold with a reference measure $\nu$ of finite *mass* $\nu(\manifold)$. A
compact manifold always has one, since a measure with a continuous density in every chart is finite
on compact sets. The uniform distribution on $\manifold$ is the law with the constant density

$$
	\density(\point) = \frac{1}{\nu(\manifold)}, \qquad \logterm(\point) = -\log \nu(\manifold) , @tag(log-density)
$$

with respect to $\nu$: the law $\nu / \nu(\manifold)$. It has no parameters. It is uniform with
respect to $\nu$ and to nothing else, so its meaning is that of the reference measure. On a compact
Lie group with Haar measure it is the one law that a translation on either side leaves unchanged,
and on a sphere with surface measure the one law that every rotation of the sphere leaves
unchanged. A measure of infinite mass has no uniform distribution: $\R^\dimension$ has none, nor
has the group of rigid motions, whose translations are not compact.

The masses of the spaces of [the chart volumes' table](../chart-volume/#the-usual-charts), each
with the measure its retraction normalizes, are these.

| space | measure | $\nu(\manifold)$ |
| --- | --- | --- |
| $S^n \subset \R^{n+1}$ | surface measure | $2 \pi^{(n+1)/2} / \Gamma\bigl(\tfrac{n+1}{2}\bigr)$: $4\pi$ for $S^2$, $2\pi^2$ for $S^3$ |
| unit quaternions $S^3$ | Haar measure, $8$ times surface measure | $16 \pi^2$ |
| rotations $SO(3)$ | Haar measure | $8 \pi^2$ |
| a product $\manifold_1 \times \cdots \times \manifold_k$ | the product measure | $\prod_i \nu_i(\manifold_i)$ |
| a quotient by a free action of $m$ elements | the image measure, divided by $m$ | $\nu(\manifold) / m$ |

The mass of the rotations is half that of the unit quaternions, which cover them twice
([%full](statement:math/chart-volume/quotient)), and it is also the integral of the Cayley chart's
volume, $\int_{\R^3} (1 + \lvert \tangent \rvert^2 / 4)^{-2}\, \dd \tangent = 8 \pi^2$, as
[](eq:math/chart-volume/image) with $g = 1$ says it must be. A uniform point of a product is a
tuple of independent uniform points of its factors, since a constant density is a product of
constants. The image of a uniform point under a quotient map is uniform on the quotient, since the
sum of a constant density over an orbit is constant. A finite set with counting measure fits the
same definition, with the number of its elements as its mass.

## In a chart

A point drawn from a normal is a mean moved by Gaussian noise through its chart. A uniform point is
any point moved through its chart by noise of another law, whose density is the chart's volume.

<Statement {...tangent} />

<Proof>

By [](eq:math/chart-volume/image) with $g = 1$, $\int_{U_\mu} v_\mu = \nu(V_\mu) = \nu(\manifold)$,
the chart being full, so $f$ is a probability density. By
[%full](statement:math/chart-volume/pushforward), $\mu \oplus \varepsilon$ has the density $f / v_\mu
= 1 / \nu(\manifold)$ on $V_\mu$ and zero on its complement, which has measure zero: that is the
uniform density. Conversely, if $\randomPoint$ is uniform it lies in $V_\mu$ almost surely, and for
a Borel set $A \subseteq U_\mu$, $\Pr(\randomPoint \ominus \mu \in A) = \nu(R_\mu(A)) /
\nu(\manifold) = \int_A v_\mu / \nu(\manifold)$, by the definition of the volume.

</Proof>

The centre $\mu$ is arbitrary: every chart describes the same law, and the tangent coordinate of a
uniform point has the same density at every centre whenever the volume does not depend on it.

For the usual global charts that density is a familiar one. The *Student t* distribution on
$\R^\dimension$ with $n$ degrees of freedom and scale $s$ has the density proportional to
$(1 + \lvert \tangent \rvert^2 / n s^2)^{-(n + \dimension)/2}$, and with $n = 1$ it is the Cauchy
distribution. The volumes of the Cayley and stereographic charts are powers of $1 + \lvert \tangent
\rvert^2 / c$, and matching the exponent and the constant gives the law of the tangent coordinate
of a uniform point.

| space, chart | $v_\mu(\tangent)$ | law of $\randomPoint \ominus \mu$ for uniform $\randomPoint$ |
| --- | --- | --- |
| $SO(3)$, Cayley | $(1 + \tfrac14 \lvert \tangent \rvert^2)^{-2}$ | Cauchy on $\R^3$ with scale $2$ |
| $S^2$, stereographic | $(1 + \tfrac14 \lvert \tangent \rvert^2)^{-2}$ | Student t on $\R^2$ with $2$ degrees of freedom and scale $\sqrt 2$ |
| $S^3$, stereographic | $(1 + \tfrac1{16} \lvert \tangent \rvert^2)^{-3}$ | Student t on $\R^3$ with $3$ degrees of freedom and scale $4 / \sqrt 3$ |

So a uniform rotation is a Cauchy vector pushed through the Cayley chart, at any centre.

A Student t vector is a normal vector with a random scale: $\varepsilon = s \zeta / \sqrt{w / n}$
with $\zeta$ standard normal on $\R^\dimension$ and $w$ independent of it with the $\chi^2$ law of
$n$ degrees of freedom. Given $w$, $\varepsilon$ is normal with the covariance $\sigma^2 I$, and so
the uniform distribution is a mixture of the normals $\mathcal N(\mu, \sigma^2 I)$ of the chart
over their scale,

$$
	\frac{\nu}{\nu(\manifold)} = \E\bigl[\mathcal N(\mu, \sigma^2 I)\bigr], \qquad \sigma = \frac{s}{\sqrt{w / n}}, \quad w \sim \chi^2_n . @tag(mixture)
$$

On the rotations $\sigma = 2 / \lvert g \rvert$ with $g$ standard normal on the line: the scale has
the median $2.97$, is below $1$ with probability $0.046$, and has no mean. The same formula has a
second reading there. The vector $\varepsilon = 2 \zeta / g$ is the Cayley coordinate
[](eq:math/chart-volume/cayley-inverse) of the unit quaternion $(g, \zeta) / \lvert (g, \zeta)
\rvert$, a standard normal vector of $\R^4$ scaled to length one, which is uniform on $S^3$ because
the normal law of $\R^4$ is invariant under its rotations.

## Drawing a uniform point

[%full](statement:tangent) is one sampler: draw the tangent vector and move any point by it.
Another draws the point directly, from as many uniform variates as the manifold has dimensions.

<Statement {...draws} />

<Proof>

1. The map $(c, \varphi) \mapsto (\sqrt{1 - c^2} \cos \varphi, \sqrt{1 - c^2} \sin \varphi, c)$ of
   $(-1, 1) \times (0, 2\pi)$ into $S^2$ has partial derivatives that are orthogonal, of lengths
   $1 / \sqrt{1 - c^2}$ and $\sqrt{1 - c^2}$, so it carries Lebesgue measure to surface measure, on
   the sphere less an arc of measure zero. The variates $(u_1, u_2)$ are mapped to $(c, \varphi)$
   by an affine map, so $(c, \varphi)$ is uniform on the rectangle, and the point on the sphere.
2. Identify $\R^4$ with $\mathbb C^2$, the point being $(a, b)$ with $a = \sqrt{1 - u_1}\,
   e^{i \varphi}$ and $b = \sqrt{u_1}\, e^{i \psi}$. In the polar coordinates of each factor,
   Lebesgue measure is $r_a\, \dd r_a\, \dd \varphi\; r_b\, \dd r_b\, \dd \psi$, and with $r_a = r
   \cos \eta$ and $r_b = r \sin \eta$ it is $r^3\, \dd r \cdot \cos \eta \sin \eta\, \dd \eta\,
   \dd \varphi\, \dd \psi$. Lebesgue measure is also $r^3\, \dd r$ times surface measure, so
   surface measure is $\cos \eta \sin \eta\, \dd \eta\, \dd \varphi\, \dd \psi = \tfrac12\, \dd
   \rho\, \dd \varphi\, \dd \psi$ with $\rho = \sin^2 \eta = \lvert b \rvert^2$. It has a constant
   density in $(\rho, \varphi, \psi) = (u_1, 2 \pi u_2, 2 \pi u_3)$, so the point is uniform on
   $S^3$, and its image is uniform on the rotations, as the image of a uniform point under a
   quotient map is.

</Proof>

The first is Archimedes' theorem, that the height of a uniform point of the sphere is uniform, and
the second is Shoemake's method for uniform rotations. On a sphere of any dimension a standard
normal vector of $\R^{n+1}$ scaled to length one is uniform, which is Muller's method, by the
invariance used above, at the cost of one variate more than the sphere has dimensions. A uniform point of a
product is drawn factor by factor.

## A normal with a large scale

The [normal](../normal/) $\mathcal N(\mu, \Sigma)$ of a manifold with a global chart is the law of
$\mu \oplus \varepsilon$ for $\varepsilon$ normal on $\R^\dimension$. On $\R^\dimension$ a large
$\Sigma$ spreads it thinly over everything. On a compact manifold it does the opposite.

<Statement {...broad} />

<Proof>

The density of $\varepsilon_k$ is at most $(2\pi)^{-\dimension/2} (\det \Sigma_k)^{-1/2}$, its value
at $0$, so for a bounded Borel set $B$, $\Pr(\varepsilon_k \in B) \le \operatorname{vol}(B)\,
(2\pi)^{-\dimension/2} (\det \Sigma_k)^{-1/2} \to 0$. If $K \subseteq V_\mu$ is compact, so is $B =
R_\mu^{-1}(K)$, the inverse of the chart being continuous on $V_\mu$, and $\Pr(\randomPoint_k \in K)
= \Pr(\varepsilon_k \in B)$.

Let $\manifold$ be compact and the chart full, and let $\eta > 0$. The open set $V_\mu$ is a
countable union of compact sets and $\nu(V_\mu) = \nu(\manifold)$ is finite, so there is a compact
$K \subseteq V_\mu$ with $\nu(K) \ge (1 - \eta)\, \nu(\manifold)$. The uniform distribution gives
$K$ at least $1 - \eta$ and the law of $\randomPoint_k$ gives it a probability tending to $0$, so
the total variation distance is eventually at least $1 - 2\eta$. Finally, if a subsequence of the
laws converges weakly to $P$, then for every open $O$ whose closure is a compact subset of $V_\mu$,
$P(O) \le \liminf_k \Pr(\randomPoint_k \in O) = 0$, and $V_\mu$ is a countable union of such sets,
so $P(V_\mu) = 0$.

</Proof>

The proof uses nothing of the manifold but the chart: the mass of a broad normal goes to infinity in
the tangent space, as it does on $\R^\dimension$, and a global chart of a compact manifold sends
infinity to the few points it does not reach. Those are the half-turns from $\mu$ for the Cayley
chart of the rotations, and the antipode $-\mu$ for the stereographic chart of a sphere. With an
isotropic scale $\sigma$ the limit is explicit. The direction of $\varepsilon$ is uniform and
independent of its length, which tends to infinity, so on the rotations $\mathcal N(\mu, \sigma^2
I)$ converges to the law of $\mu$ turned by a half-turn about a uniformly distributed axis, and on
the sphere to the point mass at $-\mu$.

On the rotations with the Cayley chart, the angle between a draw and its mean is $\theta = 2
\arctan(\sigma \chi / 2)$, with $\chi$ the length of a standard normal vector of $\R^3$, while the
angle of a uniform rotation from any fixed one has the density $(1 - \cos \theta) / \pi$ on $[0,
\pi]$, with the median $2.31$ rad.

| $\sigma$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $2.4$ | $3$ | $10$ | $100$ | uniform |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| median angle, rad | $0.73$ | $1.31$ | $2.15$ | $2.32$ | $2.88$ | $3.12$ | $2.31$ |
| total variation from uniform | $0.87$ | $0.65$ | $0.37$ | $0.38$ | $0.65$ | $0.93$ | $0$ |

No isotropic normal on the rotations is closer to uniform than $0.37$ in total variation, which
the scale $\sigma = 2.4$ attains, and the scale whose median angle is that of the uniform
distribution, near $3$, puts that angle on a shell around the mean and not, as the uniform
distribution does, in a law with no centre.

**Where the modes are.** Read in the chart at $\mu$, the log-density
[](eq:math/normal/log-density) is $-\tfrac12 \lvert W \tangent \rvert^2 - \lambda_\mu(\tangent)$ up
to a constant, with the Hessian $-\Lambda - \nabla^2 \lambda_\mu(0)$ at $\tangent = 0$. For the
Cayley chart of the rotations and the stereographic chart of $S^2$, $\nabla^2 \lambda_\mu(0) = -I$,
so $\mu$ is a strict local maximum of the density when every eigenvalue of $\Sigma$ is below $1$,
and is not a local maximum when one of them is above it. With an isotropic scale $\sigma > 1$ the
maxima are the points with $1 + \lvert \tangent \rvert^2 / 4 = \sigma^2$, a shell of rotations by
the angle $\theta$ with $\cos(\theta / 2) = 1 / \sigma$: $1.68$ rad at $\sigma = 1.5$ and $2.46$ rad
at $\sigma = 3$.

**No chart mends this.** A global chart is covered by [%full](statement:broad), whichever it is. A
chart that is not global, such as the exponential chart, wraps a broad normal around the manifold
instead, and on the rotations that does not give the uniform distribution either: the length of
$\varepsilon$ taken modulo $2\pi$ tends to a uniform law on the circle, so the angle of the rotation
tends to a uniform law on $[0, \pi]$, with the median $\pi / 2$, and not to the density $(1 - \cos
\theta) / \pi$. Wrapping gives the uniform distribution on a torus, where the exponential is a
group homomorphism, and not in general.

## Laws that do reach the uniform

Two families of laws around a point $\mu$ contain the uniform distribution or tend to it, and
neither is the normal.

**Student t noise.** By [%full](statement:tangent) the law of $\mu \oplus \varepsilon$ for
$\varepsilon$ a Student t vector is, for one number of degrees of freedom and one scale, the uniform
distribution exactly, and with many degrees of freedom it is nearly a normal. Its density with
respect to $\nu$ is the t density over the chart's volume. On the rotations, with Cauchy noise of
scale $s$, that is proportional to $(1 + r^2/4)^2 / (1 + r^2/s^2)^2$ in the chart's radius $r$,
which rises from $1$ at the mean to $(s/2)^4$ at the half-turns when $s > 2$: a scale above the
uniform's still leans towards the half-turns, by a bounded factor.

**A Gaussian against the reference measure.** The function $\exp(-\tfrac12 \lvert W (\point \ominus
\mu) \rvert^2)$, normalized as a density with respect to $\nu$ and not to Lebesgue measure in the
chart, is the law of [the Gaussian part alone](../normal/#the-gaussian-part-alone),

$$
	\density(\point) = \frac{1}{Z} \exp\bigl(-\tfrac12 \lvert W (\point \ominus \mu) \rvert^2\bigr), \qquad Z = \int_{\R^\dimension} \exp\bigl(-\tfrac12 \lvert W \tangent \rvert^2\bigr)\, v_\mu(\tangent)\, \dd \tangent , @tag(against-measure)
$$

for a global and full chart. Its density is largest at $\mu$, at every scale. As every eigenvalue of
$\Sigma$ grows, the integrand of $Z$ increases to $v_\mu$, so $Z \to \nu(\manifold)$ and the density
tends to $1 / \nu(\manifold)$ at every point of $V_\mu$: the law tends to the uniform distribution,
in total variation. For a concentrated scale it is nearly the normal, from which it differs by the
factor $v_\mu$, close to $1$ near the mean. It has two costs. Its normalizer depends on the scale
through an integral that has a closed form only in special cases; on the rotations with the Cayley
chart and an isotropic scale,

$$
	Z(\sigma) = 16 \pi^2 \Bigl( \bigl(\tfrac12 + a\bigr)\, e^{a} \operatorname{erfc}\bigl(\sqrt a\bigr) - \sqrt{a / \pi} \Bigr), \qquad a = \frac{2}{\sigma^2} , @tag(normalizer)
$$

which is $(2\pi)^{3/2} \sigma^3$ to leading order as $\sigma \to 0$ and $8\pi^2$ as $\sigma \to
\infty$. And its draw is not a point moved by noise of a known law: it is made by rejection, either
from a normal $\varepsilon$ accepted with probability $v_\mu(\varepsilon)$, which succeeds with
probability $Z / (2\pi)^{\dimension/2} (\det \Sigma)^{1/2}$, or from a uniform point accepted with
probability $\exp(-\tfrac12 \lvert W (\point \ominus \mu) \rvert^2)$, which succeeds with probability
$Z / \nu(\manifold)$. On the rotations these are $0.43$ and $0.085$ at $\sigma = 1$, $0.070$ and
$0.38$ at $\sigma = 3$, and $0.0037$ and $0.73$ at $\sigma = 10$. Either spends a random number of
variates on a draw.
